\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless stated otherwise, results are for Bernoulli assignment and $\sigma=0.5$, on the synthetic graphs of Section~\ref{sec:design}; intervals are Monte Carlo intervals that treat graph draws as the unit of replication. All outcomes are simulated from the mechanisms of Table~\ref{tab:mech}.

\subsection{Variance follows leverage; bias does not}
\label{sec:varbias}

\paragraph{Variance.}
The sampling variance of the regression estimator follows $\sigma^2L/n$. Over the 42 cells of the $n$, $d$ and $p$ slices, log RMSE falls with $n$ with slope $-0.50$, rises with $1+d$ with slope $+0.55$ [0.547, 0.556] and falls with $p(1-p)$ with slope $-0.50$ (the predicted slope is $\pm\tfrac12$ in each). The $d$ slope is therefore not $\tfrac12$ in these data: $(1+d)^{1/2}$ approximates $L^{1/2}$ only roughly, and the slope rests on three degrees ($d=5,20,80$); the intervals are Monte Carlo only and treat the 42 cells as independent although they share graphs and draws. The variance is smallest at $p=\tfrac12$ in all nine mechanism--degree slices that were run (Figure~\ref{fig:bias}, lower row).

\paragraph{Bias.}
The exact population-OLS value of the bias (Eq.~\ref{eq:bias}, evaluated per cell) lies inside the Monte Carlo interval of the measured bias in 82\% of 1283 cells; for mechanism (e) alone it does so in 58\% (largest gap 0.111, cause not diagnosed). The bias is independent of $d$ for the saturating, diffusion and interaction mechanisms (c), (e), (f), and not for the others: the median absolute bias over $p$ and $s$ falls from 0.115 to 0.025 to 0.007 for $d=5,20,80$ in (b), for which the bias reduces to $s[\mathrm{HM}(d_i)/\mathrm{AM}(d_i)-1]$ (Section~\ref{sec:method}), and rises for the threshold mechanism (d) from 0.17 to 0.48 and 0.45. At $p=\tfrac12$, $s=\tfrac12$ the bias of (d) is 0.242, 0.731 and 0.250, non-monotone in $d$; we read this as the observed exposure range straddling the threshold at some degrees and not at others, which we did not test separately. For the saturating mechanism the bias changes sign at $p\approx0.41$--$0.45$, as the curvature term predicts; the bias minimiser over $p$ is not informative for the linear mechanism (the minimum of $|{\rm bias}|$ there is a Monte Carlo artefact and we do not quote it), and the convex mirror (g) has too few $p$ values for the direction of the shift to be tested. Figure~\ref{fig:bias} shows bias and SD separately, with DM for reference.
